      "sequence_position": 19,
      "problem": "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ about $x=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =12x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}-8{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(1)=12\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{2}}-8\\cdot {1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{2}}=4{e}^{-1}=\\frac{4}{e}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(1)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot \\frac{4}{e}=\\frac{2}{3e}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x-1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{2}{3e}.$",
